# Product Requirements Document (PRD)
## Project: Catalog API Studio
### Version: MVP 1.0

## 1. Product Overview

**Catalog API Studio** is a local Python desktop application for digitizing supplier and manufacturer catalogs and transforming them into:

- structured product data
- REST API
- smart search

The product is intended for a **service business workflow**, not as a SaaS platform.  
A single operator receives PDF/XLSX/CSV catalogs from a client, uploads them into the application, extracts product data, reviews and corrects uncertain records, and delivers an API and searchable product dataset to the client.

---

## 2. Business Context

### 2.1 Market Problem
Many suppliers and manufacturers still store their product catalogs in:
- PDF
- Excel
- CSV
- scanned documents

These formats are inconvenient for:
- ecommerce
- ERP/CRM
- dealer portals
- API integrations
- smart search by SKU, parameters, and alternatives

### 2.2 Opportunity
There is demand for a service that transforms such catalogs into:
- clean structured data
- API
- searchable index
- integration-ready digital product catalog

### 2.3 Operating Model
The product is used by **one specialist** as an internal tool for performing client projects.

This is not a self-service client platform.  
It is a production tool for a service business.

---

## 3. Product Goals

### 3.1 Primary Goal
Build a working MVP tool that enables one specialist to deliver projects involving:
- catalog digitization
- product data extraction and normalization
- API creation
- smart search creation

### 3.2 MVP Goals
The MVP must allow the operator to:

1. upload a client catalog
2. extract product data
3. review problematic records
4. manually correct uncertain fields
5. save normalized results
6. expose REST API
7. search data through a built-in search interface

### 3.3 MVP Success Definition
The MVP is successful if it allows:
- processing at least 1 real client catalog end-to-end
- producing a usable product database
- delivering a working API to the client
- demonstrating search by SKU/name/brand/attributes
- executing a project without rebuilding the system from scratch each time

---

## 4. Target Audience

### Primary User
**Single operator / service owner**
- uploads documents
- monitors extraction
- fixes errors
- exports results
- uses API and search as client deliverables

### Secondary Audience
Indirect beneficiaries:
- manufacturers
- suppliers
- distributors
- B2B ecommerce companies
- companies with ERP/CRM/PIM needs

They are not the direct users of the MVP UI.

---

## 5. Core Use Cases

### UC1 — Catalog Import
The operator uploads:
- PDF
- XLSX
- CSV

The system stores the file and creates an import job.

### UC2 — Data Extraction
The system:
- analyzes the document
- extracts text/tables
- identifies product rows
- builds structured product records

### UC3 — Review Uncertain Records
The system shows:
- confidence score
- problematic records
- low-confidence fields

The operator manually corrects values.

### UC4 — Normalization
The system:
- cleans text
- standardizes SKU format
- extracts brand
- normalizes units
- builds a basic attribute structure

### UC5 — Search
The operator or demo user can search by:
- SKU
- partial SKU
- name
- brand
- category
- attributes

### UC6 — API
The system exposes REST API for:
- product list
- product details
- search
- brands
- categories
- filters

### UC7 — Export
The operator can export data as:
- JSON
- CSV

---

## 6. MVP Scope

## 6.1 In Scope

### Interface
- Python desktop UI
- file upload screen
- import list
- document list
- extraction results viewer
- extracted rows viewer
- manual field editing
- search screen
- API health/docs status

### Input Formats
- PDF
- XLSX
- CSV

### PDF Processing
Support 3 modes:
- native extraction
- bitmap fallback
- hybrid merge

### OCR / Layout / Tables
Open-source stack:
- PaddleOCR / PP-Structure
- Surya
- Docling (optional orchestration layer)

### Data Extraction
Minimum extracted fields:
- source
- sku
- brand
- name
- category
- description
- attributes
- unit
- price
- stock
- documents
- images

### Normalization
- text cleaning
- SKU normalization
- brand extraction
- unit normalization
- basic categorization
- simple deduplication

### Review Workflow
- confidence score
- uncertain-field flags
- manual editing

### Search
- SKU search
- partial SKU search
- name search
- brand search
- category filters
- attribute filters
- autocomplete

### API
Minimum endpoints:
- `GET /products`
- `GET /products/{id}`
- `GET /search?q=`
- `GET /brands`
- `GET /categories`
- `GET /filters`
- `GET /health`

### Storage
- SQLite for MVP or PostgreSQL-ready architecture
- local file storage for documents and previews

---

## 6.2 Out of Scope

- public SaaS client portal
- multi-tenancy
- billing
- client accounts
- advanced conversational AI assistant
- perfect automatic recognition for all PDF types
- advanced OEM/cross-reference engine
- production-grade cluster deployment
- enterprise permissions / SSO
- full-scale PIM/MDM system
- auto-training ML pipeline

---

## 7. Functional Requirements

## 7.1 File Import
The system must:
- accept PDF/XLSX/CSV
- store file and metadata
- create an import job entity
- display processing status

## 7.2 PDF Processing
The system must:
- detect whether a native text layer exists
- choose processing mode
- render pages to bitmap when needed
- store page previews

## 7.3 OCR / Layout / Tables
The system must:
- extract text from pages
- detect layout blocks
- detect tables
- reconstruct product rows at least at a basic level

## 7.4 Product Extraction
The system must build product-like records from extracted content.

Minimum fields:
- id
- source_document
- sku
- brand
- name
- category
- description
- attributes
- unit
- price
- stock
- confidence_score

## 7.5 Review
The operator must be able to:
- view extracted rows
- open a record
- edit fields
- save corrections

## 7.6 Search
The system must:
- index products
- perform text search
- filter by fields
- support autocomplete

## 7.7 API
The system must:
- return JSON
- support filtering and pagination
- publish OpenAPI docs

## 7.8 Export
The system must:
- export selected products to CSV/JSON

---

## 8. Non-Functional Requirements

### Performance
- local operation on a standard workstation
- acceptable processing of one small-to-medium catalog without freezing the UI
- background tasks must not block the UI

### Reliability
- import errors must not crash the entire application
- logging for errors and processing steps

### Maintainability
- modular architecture
- type hints
- pydantic models
- testable services

### Extensibility
- replaceable OCR backend
- pluggable LLM providers
- migration path from SQLite to PostgreSQL
- replaceable search engine

### UX
- practical operator-oriented interface
- clear statuses
- quick access to uncertain rows
- minimal unnecessary complexity

---

## 9. AI Requirements

AI is an auxiliary layer, not the sole extraction mechanism.

### AI is used for:
- difficult classification
- attribute extraction from messy text
- title normalization
- matching similar records
- resolving ambiguous cases

### AI must not:
- be the only extraction mechanism
- block usage if no cloud service is configured
- hard-lock the app to a single provider

### Requirement
The system must provide an abstraction layer for:
- Claude API
- OpenAI API
- DeepSeek API
- Qwen API

---

## 10. Technical Architecture

## 10.1 Recommended Stack
- Python 3.11+
- PySide6 or PyQt6
- FastAPI
- SQLite for MVP, architecture ready for PostgreSQL
- Typesense or OpenSearch for search
- PaddleOCR / Surya / Docling
- Pydantic
- SQLAlchemy or SQLModel
- background tasks / lightweight queue

## 10.2 Suggested Module Structure
- `app/ui`
- `app/core`
- `app/importers`
- `app/pdf`
- `app/ocr`
- `app/layout`
- `app/tables`
- `app/extraction`
- `app/normalization`
- `app/review`
- `app/search`
- `app/api`
- `app/db`
- `app/models`
- `app/services`
- `app/config`
- `tests`

---

